s\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cp\u003EThe domain is published and tested independently, then composed through cCoder.Core when an application needs the complete platform. That keeps ownership visible while preserving a straightforward application bootstrap.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch2\u003EGetting started\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe source, current README, sample hosts and tests live in \u003Ca href=\u0022https://github.com/ccoder-co-uk/cCoder.Data\u0022 target=\u0022_blank\u0022 rel=\u0022noreferrer\u0022\u003EcCoder.Data on GitHub\u003C/a\u003E. The repository README is the version-specific starting point; the outline below explains how the pieces fit together.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EReference the package\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cpre\u003E\u003Ccode class=\u0022language-xml\u0022\u003E\u0026lt;PackageReference Include=\u0022cCoder.Data\u0022 Version=\u0022YOUR_VERSION\u0022 /\u0026gt;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDuring coordinated local development the repositories may substitute a sibling \u003Ccode\u003EProjectReference\u003C/code\u003E. Published applications should normally consume the agreed package version so the dependency boundary remains explicit.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003ERegister it in the host\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cpre\u003E\u003Ccode class=\u0022language-csharp\u0022\u003Ebuilder.Services.AddCoreData(connectionString);\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EConfigure and run it\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EConnectionStrings:Core\u003C/code\u003E is required. The local Data.Web tooling host also needs the SSO connection and decryption key because the shared context applies user-aware filters.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EConsume the capability\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EInject \u003Ccode\u003ECoreDataContext\u003C/code\u003E or the domain broker built over it. For inspection and development, run the authenticated Data.Web CRUD host rather than exposing the context as a public application API.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u0