3Ch2\u003EHow it fits\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cp\u003EThe domain is published and tested independently, then composed through cCoder.Core when an application needs the complete platform. That keeps ownership visible while preserving a straightforward application bootstrap.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch2\u003EGetting started\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe source, current README, sample hosts and tests live in \u003Ca href=\u0022https://github.com/ccoder-co-uk/ccoder.Eventing\u0022 target=\u0022_blank\u0022 rel=\u0022noreferrer\u0022\u003Eccoder.Eventing on GitHub\u003C/a\u003E. The repository README is the version-specific starting point; the outline below explains how the pieces fit together.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EReference the package\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cpre\u003E\u003Ccode class=\u0022language-xml\u0022\u003E\u0026lt;PackageReference Include=\u0022cCoder.Eventing\u0022 Version=\u0022YOUR_VERSION\u0022 /\u0026gt;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDuring coordinated local development the repositories may substitute a sibling \u003Ccode\u003EProjectReference\u003C/code\u003E. Published applications should normally consume the agreed package version so the dependency boundary remains explicit.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003ERegister it in the host\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cpre\u003E\u003Ccode class=\u0022language-csharp\u0022\u003Ebuilder.Services.AddEventing();\u0026#10;builder.Services.AddEventingForType\u0026lt;OrderApproved\u0026gt;();\u0026#10;\u0026#10;// Choose transport when the event crosses the process boundary:\u0026#10;builder.Services.AddHttpEventingWeb(/* options */);\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EConfigure and run it\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn-process delivery needs only the core Eventing package. HTTP and Azure Service Bus providers have separate packages and configuration; the application chooses transport while domain code continues to publish typed facts.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EConsume the capability\u003C/h3\u003E\n