\u003EHow it fits\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cp\u003EThe domain is published and tested independently, then composed through cCoder.Core when an application needs the complete platform. That keeps ownership visible while preserving a straightforward application bootstrap.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch2\u003EGetting started\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe source, current README, sample hosts and tests live in \u003Ca href=\u0022https://github.com/ccoder-co-uk/cCoder.Mail\u0022 target=\u0022_blank\u0022 rel=\u0022noreferrer\u0022\u003EcCoder.Mail on GitHub\u003C/a\u003E. The repository README is the version-specific starting point; the outline below explains how the pieces fit together.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EReference the package\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cpre\u003E\u003Ccode class=\u0022language-xml\u0022\u003E\u0026lt;PackageReference Include=\u0022cCoder.Mail\u0022 Version=\u0022YOUR_VERSION\u0022 /\u0026gt;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDuring coordinated local development the repositories may substitute a sibling \u003Ccode\u003EProjectReference\u003C/code\u003E. Published applications should normally consume the agreed package version so the dependency boundary remains explicit.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003ERegister it in the host\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cpre\u003E\u003Ccode class=\u0022language-csharp\u0022\u003Ebuilder.Services.AddMailWeb(/* provider and platform configuration */);\u0026#10;app.StartMailWeb();\u0026#10;\u0026#10;// Sender/receiver workers use AddMailHostedServices and StartMailHostedServices.\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EConfigure and run it\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECreate MailServer records and register named SMTP, POP3 or Microsoft Graph sender/receiver providers. Keep credentials in deployment configuration or secret storage rather than page or workflow definitions.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EConsume the capability\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EApplications enqueue QueuedEmail records; the hosted sender owns delivery